  "notes": [
    "Prices are US dollars per million tokens, the unit every provider publishes.",
    "Costs are whole micro-dollars, so both tracks compare integers instead of floats.",
    "One token at $1.00 per million costs exactly one micro-dollar, which is why the",
    "arithmetic is tokens * price with no scaling factor.",
    "Round with floor(x + 0.5), not the language's round(). Python's round() is",
    "banker's rounding and would disagree with JavaScript's on the halves-round-up case."
  ],
